From 512d147b0a099fa9261b35d02072820771f6682e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 09:08:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox: a jail can push over SSH -- host:22 entries
 and the operator's agent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next §6.9, the Zeo half. ai-jail 3.7.1-r1 tunnels ssh through its
egress proxy for an allowlisted `host:22`, with strict host keys
filtered from the user's known_hosts. Zeo now lets a jail use that:

- An allowed host may name one port: `github.com:22`. validate_host
  accepts `name:PORT` (1-65535) with the name under its usual rules,
  and its errors quote the whole entry. A bare name keeps ai-jail's 443
  and 80. An ai-jail whose `--help` lacks `HOST[:PORT]` would read the
  entry as a host name, opening nothing or every port, so a project
  with such an entry refuses to start on it (`host_ports_unsupported`).
- `agent_sandbox.ssh_agent_socket`: the socket of an ssh-agent the
  operator starts for the jails alone (`ssh-agent -a
  /tmp/zeo-ssh/agent.sock`, then `ssh-add -c -t 1h -h github.com`).
  Every jail gets its folder read-only and SSH_AUTH_SOCK pointing at it,
  never the desktop agent or ~/.ssh. Refused, with its own sentence:
  not absolute, nothing listening, someone else's socket or folder, a
  folder open to others (not 700), or a folder the sandbox never maps
  (home, the runtime directory, ...).
- The kill switch empties that agent (`ssh-add -D`) with the jail: the
  agent is the operator's process, so it is disarmed, not killed.
- The socket is part of the settings fingerprint: a change asks for a
  restart.

The strings are in the nine languages.

Tests: jail_ssh.rs (the socket checks), validate_tests.rs (a name with
one port, and the refused port shapes), and the kill switch's test
with no agent.
